  "query_preview": "..Storms have dissipated over southeast Arizona, but\nwell to the south a strong MCS is moving through Hermosillo in\nSonora. Skies are mostly clear with temperatures in Tucson still\nin the low to even mid 90s for some locations. High temperatures\ntoday will be similar to yesterday running 8-12 degrees above\nnormal with the Tucson airport making another run at 110\ndegrees. Current NBM probabilities are around 20 percent to hit\n110 at KTUS. The Extreme Heat Warning continues today through 8\nPM.\n\nAloft the center of the high will shift slightly north with\nstorm coverage and chances increasing compared to yesterday.\nPlenty of moisture will be in place with PWAT values 1.2\"-1.6\"\nand surface dewpoints around 60 degrees. MLCAPE values will be \naround 500-1000 J/kg and mid-level flow will be slightly \nelevated around 15-20 knots especially along the international \nborder. Model soundings also show inverted V profiles and this \nalong with all of the other parameters will lead to a severe \nwind threat this afternoon and evening. SPC has a marginal risk \nfor severe weather today for most of southeast Arizona. Hi-res \nguidance shows storms initiating over the higher terrain of \nGraham and Greenlee Counties and along the international border \nearly to mid-afternoon. Storms then propagate along outflows \ninto the lower deserts late afternoon into the evening. Tucson \nwill likely see its first storms around 4-6 pm. Blowing dust \nwill also likely be an issue along I-10 in Cochise County during\nthe afternoon and in Pinal County late in the afternoon into \nthe evening. Storms will also be capable of heavy rainfall \nleading to isolated areas of flash flooding. The best chances \nfor heavy rainfall look to be south of Tucson with the wind \nthreat more widespread. \n\nSunday expect another round of scattered thunderstorms following\na similar evolution to today. However mid-level winds are a bit\nweaker, generally 5-10 kts, so the severe wind threat is\ndiminished some. Storm motion will likely be a bit slower\nleading to a greater flash flooding threat, especially south of\nTucson. The HREF has a small area over Santa Cruz county of 40 \nkm neighborhood probabilities of 10 percent for 3 inches of \nrain in 3 hours. \n\nGoing into the work week the ridge stays in a favorable place\nfor daily afternoon and evening thunderstorms. Coverage of \nstorms may decrease some after Wednesday as the ridge moves \nslightly southward and increases in strength, but still expect \nsome activity due to plentiful moisture and intense daytime \nheating. High temperatures will drop back to around normal by \nMonday then back to 3-8 degrees above normal Wednesday into the \nend of the work week.",
